### Strings for the computation layer documentation pages


### 3296 English words, 13 English strings

cl-docs-description-conditionals =
    
    <1>
      Computation Layer doesn't have "if," "then," or "else." Instead we use a
      structure similar to a "switch" in other programming languages. We call it
      <2>when</2>{"."} It is a list of tests, each followed by what should happen
      in that situation, and requires a default value (<3>otherwise</3>) if none of
      the conditions match.
    </1>
    <conditionals-1/>
    <4>
      Note that a "when" returns as soon as it finds a matching condition. As a
      result, when we get to the 4th condition to check whether n is greater than
      30, we already know that n is at least 10 and at most 100. This might help
      you write shorter and cleaner conditions.
    </4>
cl-docs-description-correctness =
    
    <1>
      Checking student responses for correctness is a great way to both provide
      students with feedback and help teachers quickly see correctness in the
      teacher dashboard.
    </1>
    <2>
      In general, we recommend being <3>very</3> careful when adding correctness
      checks to an activity. Computers are pretty dumb and might mark work wrong
      when a human would mark it correct, which is a really demoralizing experience
      for students. Here are a few tips for writing correctness.
    </2>
    <4>Compare Values</4>
    <5>
      If you want to check that a student got a number right, you can compare its
      value. This is generally safe if the answer is an integer, but risky
      otherwise! Always test it out yourself with a few values you expect to work.
      Here's a very simple example of checking whether the value is greater than 7:
    </5>
    <correct-1/><6>We can reuse that computation to create a feedback string in a
    note:</6><correct-2/><7>Use the Graphing Calculator</7>
    <8>
      For more complicated examples of correctness, one nice technique is to use
      the graphing calculator. We like to use movable points with a step set on
      sliders to enable snapping.
    </8>
    <9>
      In this example, we want a slope of 2. The graph computes the slope using a
      variable called <10>m</10>, and then uses snapping. The Computation Layer is
      just this line:
    </9>
    <correct-3/><11>Use Conditionals</11>
    <12>
      For more advanced correctness in the calculator, you can use conditionals.
      Build in a little tolerance for extra safety (e.g.,<13>C={"{"}|a-7.4| \u2264 0.1:
      1, 0{"}"}</13>).
    </12>
    <14>
      Most importantly, always try your correctness checks yourself with a range of
      inputs before giving them to students!
    </14>
cl-docs-section-advanced =
    
    <1>
      In this section, we'll go deep into some of the advanced ways that you can
      use Computation Layer. Three pieces of advice before you dive in:
    <2>
    
    <3>
          Always test out your creations thoroughly before using them with
          students. Focus on the interesting right and wrong answers your students
          may produce, and make sure you like what the script does in each of those
          cases.
    </3>
    <4>
          Ask for help. There's folks who will be excited to help on the <5>CL
          Forum</5>, and on Twitter (try hashtag #improveMyAB).
    </4>
    <6>
          Look at what other folks have made! You can see the CL script of any
          Desmos activity by clicking "copy and edit" on it.
    </6>
    </2>  
      Play around, have fun, and let us know what you're able to create!
    </1>
cl-docs-section-advanced-aggregate =
    
    <1>
      For any source that returns a "number" you can use the "aggregate" function
      to generate a numberList, where each element is the number from a different
      student in the class. This is a powerful feature for social classrooms, but
      also challenging to use, test, and debug. Use with caution, and make sure to
      test (we suggest using multiple incognito windows) before using with any
      students.
    </1>
    <2>
      It's easiest to see how aggregate works with a few examples. In this first
      one, we'll make a dot plot using the numbers from everyone in the class:
    </2>
    <aggregate-1/>
    <3>
      For a more complicated example, we could aggregate the coordinates of the
      submitted points for everyone in the class. For this, we'll make use of an
      action button's "capture" sink + "last value" source to only include students
      who have pressed the button.
    </3>
    <4>Here's the CL script for the button (named btn1):</4><aggregate-2/><5>And
    here's the script for the graph (graph1):</5><aggregate-3/>
cl-docs-section-advanced-interpreting-math =
    
    <1>
      We love when students can express themselves in a variety of ways.
      Unfortunately, this means extra work for you in building CL scripts that can
      interpret all of that flexibility. This section can hopefully provide some
      useful ideas. But first, some principles we like to adhere to:
    <2>
        <3>Never incorrectly mark a student wrong!</3><4>Try to interpret
        everything students might type.</4>
    <5>
          Always test your scripts by trying out lots of student responses before
          sharing with students.
    </5>
    </2>  
      For example, we almost always prefer to look at numericValue from an input
      rather than expect the exact characters. Not only does this avoid creating
      bugs when, for example, a student inadvertently types a space, it also
      invites and celebrates writing expressions (e.g., 4+5) instead of just
      numbers (e.g., "9").
    </1>
    <6>Example 1: simpleFunction</6>
    <7>
      One powerful way to interpret math is to use our graphing calculator. But
      this can be challenging. Suppose we want a student to write the equation of a
      line that passes through the point (1, 3). Here, you could make use of
      "simpleFunction." That will understand an equation of the form y=3x, or
      f(x)=3x, or even just 3x. Here's the script in the graph:
    </7>
    <interpreting-math-1/><8>And here's the script on the
    input:</8><interpreting-math-2/><9>Example 2: parseEquation</9>
    <10>
      Pretty good! But unfortunately simpleFunction requires a line to be written
      in explicit form. A line like x+y=4 is correct, but simpleFunction doesn't
      detect it! Another option is to parse the equation and look at the difference
      function. This one's subtle, so we recommend looking at the graph. This will
      turn the student equation of x+y=4 into f(x,y) = 4-(x+y). Here's what this
      looks like in the graph's CL script:
    </10>
    <interpreting-math-3/>
    <11>
      There are other options too, like the <12>xyLine</12> method, which naturally
      understands all different forms of lines, but won't work for parabolas, for
      instance. The method of parsing you use depends on the math you're hoping
      for. Unfortunately, there's no one size fits all.
    </11>
    <13>Using Patterns</13>
    <14>
      Those first examples show how to provide feedback on student responses by
      numerically evaluating the students' responses. Sometimes you also want to
      check their exact form. For this, you can try exploring our "patterns"
      feature, which lets you parse expressions into pieces. Warning: Be very
      cautious with this feature! It's under development. It's also really easy to
      accidentally mark student thinking incorrect when it's just in an unexpected
      form.
    </14>
    <15>
      Here's an example of how to check that a response is a simple number. Note
      that in Desmos, a "number" is a decimal, a fraction, or a mixed number.
    </15>
    <interpreting-math-4/>
    <16>
      You can also pull apart different parts of their expression using patterns.
      For example, we could grab the numerator and denominator of a fraction as
      follows:
    </16>
    <interpreting-math-5/>
    <17>
      Patterns are a beta feature and you should use them carefully! We expect to
      make them easier to use over time and will be expanding our documentation as
      we do.
    </17>
cl-docs-section-advanced-random =
    
    <1>
      Computation Layer supports random numbers. Random numbers are unique but
      stable for each student. This means that page reloads won't change the random
      numbers. However, every student will see a different set of random numbers.
      You can use this, for example, to give each student a different problem:
    </1>
    <random-1/>
    <2>
      In the above example, the numbers were created in the note component, but you
      can grab them off of the note's script keyword to, for example, evaluate
      correctness on the math input:
    </2>
    <random-2/>
    <3>
      The randomGenerator can also take an argument, called a "seed." This lets you
      change which random numbers a student sees (e.g., hooking up a button to
      produce a series of random problems):
    </3>
    <random-3/>
    <4>
      Here we can again evaluate correctness. This example makes use of the
      advanced patterns feature (see <5>interpreting math</5>) to ensure that
      students are typing in only a number:
    </4>
    <random-3-1/>
    <6>
      Note that for many applications the random capabilities of the graphing
      calculator might be easier and more powerful! Random numbers in the
      calculator will behave similarly, remaining stable between page loads but
      different for different students.
    </6>
cl-docs-section-components =
    
    <1>
      Components are the heart of Computation Layer. Scripts are usually attached
      to a component through the script icon. Scripts can also be attached to an
      entire screen to control the behavior of the screen itself.
    </1>
    <2>In order to access the sources of a component, you first need to name
    it:</2><3/>
    <4>
      To add a script to a component and make use of its sinks, click the script
      icon next to the component:
    </4>
    <5/>
cl-docs-section-dynamic-text =
    
    <1>
      Computation Layer lets you change the text on the page in response to work
      each student did. You can update a prompt or even use your students' own work
      from a previous screen on a later one.
    </1>
    <2>
      The recommended way to dynamically use strings in Notes is to define a local
      variable in the computation layer for the script, and then insert it into the
      content by clicking the {"{"}#{"}"} button. For example:
    </2>
    <dynamic-text-1/>
    <3>
      You can also overwrite the contents of a note wholesale using its "content"
      sink. In this case, you might want to take advantage of a feature called
      "interpolation." Inside of a string, you can write <4>\${"{"}{"}"}</4> to pull
      in a variable dynamically. For example:
    </3>
    <dynamic-text-2/>
    <5>
      Note that this technique works in other places that can accept strings, for
      example in the "pointLabel" sink for graphs:
    </5>
    <dynamic-text-3/>
cl-docs-section-getting-started =
    
    <1>
      Computation Layer lets you inject information <2>into</2> a component using
      its "sinks." It lets you read information <3>out</3> of a component using its
      "sources." Let's see how it works.
    </1>
    <4>Example: Setting Initial Text</4>
    <5>
      We're going to take a normal input and give it some sentence starters. We'll
      use the "initialText" sink.
    </5>
    <6>
      <7>Step 1: </7>Add a Text Input component to a screen and open its script box:
    </6>
    <8/>
    <9>
      <10>Step 2: </10>Use the initialText sink:
    </9>
    <getting-started-1/>
    <11>
      That's it! Click the "Try it" link above, in the top-right, to play more with
      this example.
    </11>
    <12>Example: Using a Number in a Graph</12>
    <13>
      We're going to take a number and inject it into a graph to plot a line with
      that slope.
    </13>
    <14>
      <15>Step 1: </15> Create a screen with a Graph component and a Math Input
      component.
    </14>
    <16>
      <17>Step 2: </17> Name your Math Input, so that we can refer to it. For
      example, we named ours "exp1."
    </16>
    <18/>
    <19>
      <20>Step 3: </20> Click to edit the graph, and type in <21>y=mx</21>{"."} You
      can add the slider for <22>m</22> if you want; CL will overwrite it if you do.
    </19>
    <23>
      <24>Step 4: </24> Open the script window for the graph.
    </23>
    <25/>
    <26>
      <27>Step 5: </27> Use the "number" sink to tell the Graph component that the
      value of <28>m</28> should be the numericValue from the Math Input component.
      Note: this will work if the student types <29>7</29> or <30>2/3</30> or even
      <31>4+4</31>:
    </26>
    <getting-started-2/><32>Nice work! You've built your first connection between
    components.</32>
cl-docs-section-graphs-and-sketches =
    
    <1>
      Our graphing calculator is the secret engine behind a lot of Computation
      Layer's power. Sketches can benefit from this power as well, especially if
      you set the background of the sketch to be a graph.
    </1>
    <2>
      Note that these features work best when you're using the graph component
      rather than the graphing calculator. The former hides the expressions list,
      while the latter includes it. Using CL with the graphing calculator will give
      warnings because we can't make guarantees about which variables will exist
      since a student could delete or duplicatively define named variables.
    </2>
    <3>Numbers, NumberLists, and PointLabels</3>
    <4>
      There are three main ways to get data into and out of graphs: numbers,
      numberLists, and pointLabels. Numbers are a single value, numberLists are a
      list of values, and pointLabels take a string. In each case, you can only
      read and write to named values. For example, if you make a point
      <5>A=(1,n)</5>, turn on its label, and then use CL, you can both dynamically
      place and label the point like this:
    </4>
    <graph-sketches-1/>
    <6>
      Note that number and numberList overwrite the value in the graph for the
      named variable. It's okay to put dummy data in the graph, just know that
      it'll get replaced when CL runs.
    </6>
    <7>
      You can also pass in a numberList to a named variable. For example, the
      "history" sink on a button returns a numberList. You could leave a trail of
      every place that a point has been using capture/history in combination with
      number/numberList. If the graph is named "g1" and the button is named "b1",
      here's what you'd write in the button's script:
    </7>
    <graph-sketches-2/><8>And here's what you'd write in the graph's
    script:</8><graph-sketches-3/><9>Animating Graphs</9>
    <10>
      Many things in Desmos activities that look like videos are actually just
      animated graphs. To add a play button on top of a graph, just use the
      <11>animationDuration</11> sink. That sets how long the animation will play.
      You can then read off the animationTime sink and inject that straight into
      the graph. For example:
    </10>
    <graph-sketches-4/><12>Interpreting Sketches</12>
    <13>
      At first glance, it might seem like sketches are similar to free response
      text and that there isn't anything interesting to extract from them. But
      sketches are full of rich data that you can connect to other representations.
    </13>
    <14>
      For example, you can read off the number of strokes and points. You can also
      grab every value of a sketch at a given x-value. You can combine all of these
      features together to make a lightweight version of <15>Function
      Carnival</15>{"."} Start by making a graph (g1) and sketch (s1).
    </14>
    <16>Add this script for the graph:</16><graph-sketches-5/><17>And add this
    script for the sketch:</17><graph-sketches-6/><18>Layers and
    Transformations</18>
    <19>
      You can add layers behind graphs and sketches using the "background" sink.
      Combine this with transformations that you can do on sketches to build some
      fun interactives.
    </19>
    <20>
      For example, you could have students sketch something and then animate their
      sketch offscreen when they press a button:
    </20>
    <graph-sketches-7/>
cl-docs-section-sinks-and-sources =
    
    <1>
      CL might look different from other programming languages you're used to.
      Instead of operating as a list of commands that run one after another, CL
      allows you to wire different pieces together to keep their values consistent.
      For example, <2>a = b</2> means that <3>a</3> is <4>always</4> the value of
      <5>b</5>{"."} The value of <6>a</6> will change whenever the value of
      <7>b</7> does. This is similar to how spreadsheets work. It's also how the
      Desmos calculator works.
    </1>
    <8>
      "Sinks" and "Sources" are the way to get values into and out of components.
      For example, the statement <9>number("m"): exp1.numericValue</9> means that
      the value of <10>m</10> inside of this Graph component equals the computed
      value of the Expression Input component named "exp1." As a student types into
      that Expression Input, the value of <11>m</11> in the graph will update
      immediately.
    </8>
    <12>Sinks</12>
    <13>
      Use sinks to inject values into components. Sinks are colored teal and are
      always followed by a colon. For example, you could make a graph that plots
      <14>y=mx+b</14> and then use the graph's "number" sink to set values for the
      variables <15>m</15> and <16>b</16>{"."}<sinks-and-sources-1/>
    </13>
    <17>Variables</17>
    <18>
      You can define variables within a script. This makes your code more reusable
      and helps you avoid repeating the same code. Note that variables look very
      similar to sinks but behave very differently.
    </18>
    <19>
      Suppose you wanted the submitLabel and initialText on an input to both say,
      "Hello world!"
    </19>
    <variables-1/>
    <20>
      Pro tip: You can reference variables defined in <21>other</21> components by
      using the "script" keyword. For example:
    </20>
    <variables-2/><22>Sources</22>
    <23>
      A source is a value that you can pull out of a component and is typically
      based on work a student has done. To use a component's source, name the
      component and the use that name in your script.
    </23>
    <24>
      For example, let's say that you have a button that you've named
      <25>btn1</25>{"."} You can use its sources in any script anywhere in the
      activity, like this:
    </24>
    <about-cl-5/>
    <26>
      Pro tip: If you want to access a source from a component you're in, you don't
      need to name the component. In a button Component's script, for example, just
      write <27>label: this.pressCount</27>{"."}
    </26>
cl-docs-section-types =
    
    <1>
      Every value in the Computation Layer has a type. For example, consider some
      of the sources and sinks for the Math Input component:
    <2>
    
    <3>
          The <4>timeSinceSubmit</4> source produces a number
    </3>
    <5>
          The <6>latex</6> source produces the LaTeX value of the component
    </5>
    <7>
          The <8>submitLabel</8> sink expects a string value
    </7>
    <9>
          The <10>hidden</10> sink expects a true/false value
    </9>
    </2>
    </1>
    <11>
      These examples demonstrate some basic types: numbers, LaTeX, strings, and
      booleans. However there are other types like sketches, numberLists, colors,
      etc. Abstractly, a type describes what kind of data is stored in a value, and
      what operations are available for that value. For example, the numberList
      type stores a list of numbers, and some of the available operations are to
      find the length of the list, or to get an element at a specific index.
    </11>
    <12>
      Computation Layer is a "Strongly typed" language. Every expression produces a
      value of some type. For example,<13>sketch1.sketch.pointCount</13> produces a
      value of type number, while the subexpression <14>sketch1.sketch</14>
      produces a value of type sketch. Whenever you use a value(e.g., in an
      expression, function call, or sink), we check that is the correct type for
      that usage, and show an error if it is not (e.g., trying to use a number in a
      sink that expects a string).
    </12>
    <15>Basic Types</15>
    <16>
      The most common types in the Computation Layer are numbers, strings, LaTeX
      and booleans.
    <17>
        <18>Numbers represent numeric values</18><19>String represent text
        values</19><20>Booleans are true/false values</20><21>LaTeX represent
        written mathematics</21>
    </17>  
      Strings are wrapped in quotes (""), while LaTeX is wrapped in backticks (\`\`).
      One way to see the difference is to observe how they look when inserted into
      a note. For example:<types-1/>
    </16>
    <22>Complex Types</22>
    <23>
      Complex types, such as sketches, colors, numberLists, etc, store a specific
      kind of data, and have a set of attributes, or member functions, which you
      can use to extract data from the type or transform it in some way.
    </23>
    <24>
      When working with one of these types, you generally need to answer these
      questions:
    <25>
        <26>What kind of information does this type represent?</26><27>How do I
        obtain values of this type?</27><28>What attributes or functions can I use
        to manipulate this type?</28><29>Where can I use this type?</29>
    </25>  
      You'll generally get these types from either a component source or a
      function, transform them by calling additional functions on them, and then
      use them in a sink or as an argument to another function.
    </24>
    <30>Utility Types</30>
    <31>
      The utility types, sketchTools, buttonStyles, and colorPalette, are designed
      to provide access to useful objects. For example, the colorPalette type
      contains standard Desmos colors that can then be used in the
      <32>initialColor</32> sink for the Sketch component. These types are obtained
      by referencing a global constant, e.g. <33>colors</33> for the colorPalette
      type.
    </31>
cl-docs-section-welcome =
    <1>Welcome to Computation Layer!</1>
    <2>
      Thousands of teachers use Computation Layer (CL) to add an extra layer of
      interactivity and feedback to their activities. We at Desmos use it in every
      one of our activities.
    </2>
    <3>
      CL is the glue that holds activities together. It lets you connect
      representations, customize content, and provide dynamic, interpretive
      feedback.
    </3>
    <4>
      These docs are filled with examples to help you get started. You can also
      click the script icon in the corner of any component to try it yourself.
    </4>
    <5/>
    <6>
      If you have any questions, visit our discussion forum at
      <7>cl.desmos.com</7>, reach out on <8>Twitter</8>, or check out one of our
      <9>Professional Learning</9> events. Happy building!
    </6>
